WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:08.214
You know the feeling. Too many windows, too many screens... and a morning lost to dragging.

00:00:09.564 --> 00:00:15.909
And your AI agent? It can't even see your screen — let alone show you where to click.

00:00:17.259 --> 00:00:21.342
So... what if it could just — do it?

00:00:22.692 --> 00:00:28.682
Meet windowctl. One tiny binary for your whole desktop.
npm install -g @muthuishere/windowctl

00:00:29.432 --> 00:00:35.197
First — what's open? Every window, every app, one command.
$ windowctl windows list --app Finder

00:00:35.947 --> 00:00:40.215
Your displays, too — even the one you're looking at.
$ windowctl monitors list

00:00:40.965 --> 00:00:46.423
Now: TextEdit left, Finder right. No dragging.
$ windowctl move --app TextEdit --monitor 1 --zone 1A

00:00:47.173 --> 00:00:51.677
Quarters? It just... fits.
$ windowctl move --app Finder --monitor 1 --zone 2D

00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:56.183
Thirds, any split — you name the shape.
$ windowctl move --app TextEdit --monitor 1 --zone 3:1

00:00:56.933 --> 00:01:00.580
Or exact pixels, when it matters.
$ windowctl move --app Finder --monitor 1 --x 700 --y 150 --w 900 --h 600

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.796
Resize — hands off the mouse.
$ windowctl resize --app Finder --w 640 --h 480

00:01:05.546 --> 00:01:09.258
Launch an app, and wait till it's really there.
$ windowctl launch --app Calculator && windowctl wait --app Calculator

00:01:10.008 --> 00:01:12.525
Bring anything forward.
$ windowctl focus --app TextEdit

00:01:13.275 --> 00:01:20.283
And my favourite — a whole layout in one call. Just like that, everything's home.
$ echo '[{app,zone}, …]' | windowctl batch

00:01:21.033 --> 00:01:25.745
It can see, too. One window, one screenshot...
$ windowctl screenshot --app Calculator --out calc.png

00:01:26.495 --> 00:01:32.599
...every pixel's a real screen point, so the mouse lands right on a button...
$ windowctl mouse move --x -830 --y 769

00:01:33.349 --> 00:01:35.741
...click. Seven.
$ windowctl mouse click --x -830 --y 769

00:01:36.491 --> 00:01:40.922
It types — but only after checking the window really has focus.
$ windowctl type --app TextEdit --text "windowctl typed this"

00:01:41.672 --> 00:01:43.753
Shortcuts, too.
$ windowctl key --app TextEdit --combo "cmd+left"

00:01:44.503 --> 00:01:49.948
Now the good part. Ask for the Format menu — by name.
$ windowctl draw --app TextEdit --find Format --role menuitem --text "AX: Format menu"

00:01:50.698 --> 00:01:54.124
No labels? It just reads the screen.
$ windowctl draw --app TextEdit --find "focus-guarded" --source ocr --style ring

00:01:54.874 --> 00:01:59.118
Then it shows you. A big, friendly arrow...
$ windowctl draw --monitor 1 --at 900,560 --text "arrow + sign"

00:01:59.868 --> 00:02:03.559
...a box, or a ring, right where it matters.
$ windowctl draw … --style box   |   --style ring

00:02:04.309 --> 00:02:07.073
Any shape. Any colour.
$ windowctl draw … --shape zigzag --color purple

00:02:07.823 --> 00:02:13.644
Step by step. One: press nine. Two: press plus.
$ windowctl draw --app Calculator --find "+" --role button --text "2 · press +" --detach

00:02:14.394 --> 00:02:17.879
Done? Gone, in one go.
$ windowctl draw stop

00:02:18.629 --> 00:02:22.605
Share your screen to any browser — and drive it from there.
$ windowctl remote --monitor 1

00:02:23.355 --> 00:02:29.516
Not just a Mac thing. On Windows — same commands, same menu...
> .\windowctl draw --app notepad --find File --role menuitem --text "AX: File menu"

00:02:30.266 --> 00:02:32.084
...same eyes.
> .\windowctl draw --app notepad --find "Find this phrase" --source ocr --style ring

00:02:32.866 --> 00:02:39.320
One binary. Every screen. Go make your desktop work for you.
npm install -g @muthuishere/windowctl
